      "content": "To maintain scientific rigor without relying on biased generalizations, focus your questions on observable actions and attitudes rather than group membership. You could use scenarios that present conflict and ask respondents *how* they would react, such as \"If a neighbor was frequently causing noise disturbances, what would be your first course of action?\" For communication style, ask about preferred methods of expression, like \"When discussing an issue, do you prefer direct confrontation or indirect negotiation?\" This method probes behavior rather than profiling identity.",
